{
  "dom_id": "step-q352889",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}] & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral. We first expand the squared term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1}+x{cos}^{2}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3}))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+({cos}^{2}⁡(t)-(-{cos}^{2}⁡(t))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
